{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1302 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b4ec2fc239e3f1cb7ea516b5b5f79b602c499c594e8fe81d6800ab6abf6fb937" :edb/db-before-t 1301 :edb/db-after-t 1302 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534614 50 #inst "2026-10-08T02:30:10.217Z" 13194139534614 true] [17592186082925 1100 "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" 13194139534614 true] [17592186082925 1100 "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" 13194139534614 false] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298880 13194139534614 true] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298885 13194139534614 true] [17592186083120 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298888 13194139534614 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298866 13194139534614 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186298873 13194139534614 true] [17592186083680 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534614 true] [17592186083680 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/assets/bb30925ffaca0ce59de7508e0f3710fbb26f3a80dfbd80b689e9ee5a4cf7d699.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}$ for $x\\in [-3,4],$ as shown above?" 13194139534614 true] [17592186083680 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/760/Source/Images/q-51267.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y = \\frac{x^{2}}{4}$ for $x ∈ [- 3, 4]$, as shown above?" 13194139534614 false] [17592186083680 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $a=-3,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2x}{4}=\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534614 true] [17592186083742 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534614 true] [17592186083742 1100 "The length of a curve $y = f(x)$ is given by $∫_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1 + 9e^{4x}}dx$. Which of the following could be an equation for this curve?" 13194139534614 false] [17592186083742 1100 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+9{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139534614 true] [17592186083742 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+9{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=9{e}^{4x}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 3\\int {e}^{2x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2x}+1.$" 13194139534614 true] [17592186289367 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/assets/431975da27fb1ff06baa520ef4f870cb34c879f384bfc5ddd774422f15fe7b57.pn